    {
      "@type": "trus:ComplianceRequirement",
      "id": "trus:OSHA-REC-024",
      "requirement_text": "When an authorized employee representative requests OSHA 301 Incident Reports for a represented establishment, the employer must provide the permitted 'Tell us about the case' information within seven calendar days and remove all other information.",
      "is_mandatory": true,
      "requirement_type": "obligation",
      "risk_level": "medium",
      "verification_method": "record_review",
      "audit_evidence_type": "incident_record",
      "workflow_trigger": "always_on",
      "workflow_trigger_details": {},
      "industry_scope": "all_covered_industries",
      "industries_naics": [],
      "industry_condition": "",
      "business_activities": [
        "process_operation"
      ],
      "employee_size_band": "any",
      "jurisdictions": [
        "federal"
      ],
      "source_framework": "OSHA_RECORDKEEPING",
      "source_clause": "§1904.35(b)(2)(v)(B)",
      "supporting_clauses": [],
      "source_url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/part-1904#p-1904.35(b)(2)(v)(B)",
      "source_text_excerpt": "(B) When an authorized employee representative asks for copies of the OSHA 301 Incident Reports for an establishment where the agent represents employees under a collective bargaining agreement, you must give copies of those forms to the authorized employee representative within 7 calendar days. You are only required to give the authorized employee representative information from the OSHA 301 Incident Report section titled \"Tell us about the case.\" You must remove all other information from the copy of the OSHA 301 Incident Report or the equivalent substitute form that you give to the authorized employee representative.",
      "source_version": "2024-07-01",
      "source_retrieved_at": "2026-09-26T00:00:00Z",
      "source_text_hash": "6f7dc71377cbfd9ca52d04aa299c7a6148911b25d32bce6254096eb68a5704ed",
      "curation_method": "ai_assisted_with_human_review",
      "curator": "Eugene Sun",
      "review_date": "2026-09-27",
      "verification_status": "implemented_from_human_verification"
    },
    {
      "@type": "trus:ComplianceRequirement",
      "id": "trus:OSHA-REC-025",
      "requirement_text": "The employer must post the annual summary no later than February 1 following the covered year and keep it posted through April 30.",
      "is_mandatory": true,
      "requirement_type": "obligation",
      "risk_level": "low",
      "verification_method": "physical_inspection",
      "audit_evidence_type": "physical_condition",
      "workflow_trigger": "annual_review",
      "workflow_trigger_details": {},
      "industry_scope": "all_covered_industries",
      "industries_naics": [],
      "industry_condition": "",
      "business_activities": [
        "process_operation"
      ],
      "employee_size_band": "any",
      "jurisdictions": [
        "federal"
      ],
      "source_framework": "OSHA_RECORDKEEPING",
      "source_clause": "§1904.32(b)(6)",
      "supporting_clauses": [],
      "source_url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/part-1904#p-1904.32(b)(6)",
      "source_text_excerpt": "(6) When do I have to post the annual summary? You must post the summary no later than February 1 of the year following the year covered by the records and keep the posting in place until April 30.",
      "source_version": "2024-07-01",
      "source_retrieved_at": "2026-09-26T00:00:00Z",
